{"kind":"request","text":"Rewrite the main README around one concrete artifact-currentness story before explaining the workroom or substrate. Multiple technically capable readers independently report that they cannot tell exactly what gitseq does, whether the `document` is the ordinary artifact or the knowledge projection, or what using the `document that knows when it is wrong` feels like. The feedback is accurate: the current README opens with a generic layer-over-git claim, puts setup and an indefinite agent prompt before the product explanation, then reaches the artifact promise only after the clock, fold, and ontology discussion.\n\nThe first screen must answer in plain English: `Gitseq tells you whether a tracked artifact still rests on current decisions—and exactly what changed when it does not.` Immediately follow with one small causal diagram and one real before/after example. The diagram should show an ordinary decision or implementation artifact governing a normal repository document at an exact commit; a later effective supersession replacing that premise; the deterministic projection marking the document `Needs review`; and a reader opening the causal explanation. Label the normal file as the document, the signed artifact statement as its provenance pointer, and the projection as the computed status/index. Supply equivalent surrounding prose and descriptive text so the explanation survives clients that do not render the diagram.\n\nUse a reproducible dogfood example grounded in this repository's documentation practice, with exact commands or recorded fixture output that a reader can follow from `Current` to `Needs review` and then inspect `because X was replaced by Y`. Keep it short enough to understand in about a minute and show the ordinary artifact, its `path@commit`, the basis it rests on, the superseding event, and the resulting explanation. Do not require the not-yet-built artifact browser or fabricate a screenshot of UI that does not ship; use the current CLI/status/provenance behavior and point forward to #2816 only as forthcoming ergonomics. If a visual is added, verify its GitHub rendering and keep a maintainable source.\n\nCorrect the current overclaims. Replace `the document knows it is wrong` with language that says a tracked artifact knows when a premise moved and therefore needs re-checking; the maintained staleness contract explicitly says a flare is not a correctness verdict. Replace `every artifact` with `every tracked artifact`, and state that unanchored work is invisible. Claim section-level explanations only where sections are independently registered; otherwise the artifact/page is the unit that flares. State plainly that gitseq does not infer dependencies from files or imports and does not decide whether updated prose is correct.\n\nReorder the README so the sequence is: exact outcome; causal diagram; concrete example; what is and is not tracked; why this matters; how the three small mechanics produce it; the workroom as the first application; setup; then deeper architecture. Preserve the valuable signed-order, offline-verification, no-lock-in, technical-preview, and agent-coordination material, but move it behind the concrete model instead of making readers decode it first. Keep the main path short, link to maintained detail, use ISO 24495-1 plain English, and avoid adding another product name or a broad `tracker for anything` slogan before the concrete artifact promise.\n\nAdd a regression check appropriate to the chosen diagram/example form: commands in the example run in the documentation gate or are generated from a tested fixture; links resolve; the README does not reintroduce `wrong` as the meaning of stale, `every artifact` as automatic coverage, or projection-as-document ambiguity. Update any directly contradictory maintained landing pages in the same change, but do not rewrite the entire documentation set.","body":{"branch":"request/readme-artifact-first","conditions":"The first screen states the concrete tracked-artifact outcome before setup or architecture; one accessible causal diagram distinguishes ordinary document, signed artifact pointer, and computed projection; one reproducible current-to-needs-review dogfood example shows path@commit, basis, supersession, and because-X explanation using behavior that ships today; stale is consistently needs re-checking rather than wrong, coverage is explicitly tracked-only, section granularity is not overclaimed, and non-inference limits are clear; workroom/agent/architecture material is preserved but follows the product model; examples or generated output are gated, links and visual source are verified, directly contradictory landing prose is reconciled, and documentation plus repository gates pass.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}